  "markdown": "# Why did the failure rate of ST12000NM0008 rise in Q2 2026, and is the increase real?\n\n## Summary\n- ST12000NM0008's AFR rose to 2.97% in 2026Q2 from 1.71% in 2026Q1, with 145 failures over 1,780,606 drive-days. (ev_d6fc934af81c1958)\n- The increase is unlikely to be noise: the 2026Q2 failure rate is 1.74 times the 2026Q1 rate (95% CI 1.32 to 2.31, p = 4.9e-5). (ev_313417fa34a0f7c1)\n- It had the highest AFR of the Backblaze cohorts with at least 60 drives in 2026Q2, at 2.23 times the fleet AFR of 1.33%. (ev_90c415e7b3adc7b6, ev_a30c4c10518464eb)\n\n## Findings\n- Before 2026Q2 the cohort's AFR stayed between 1.37% and 1.71% for three quarters. (ev_d6fc934af81c1958)\n- In the SMART sample, the share of drive-days with non-zero current pending sectors (SMART 197) rose from 0.0193 in 2026Q1 to 0.0544 in 2026Q2, across 255 sampled drives. (ev_61022e1d7a535590)\n- The most recent failure in the window, drive ZL0M85OIR, failed at 2,147 days of age with 175 pending sectors on its last day. (ev_ae356114fe296998)\n- The Backblaze fleet logged 623 failures across 189,650 drives in 2026Q2. (ev_a30c4c10518464eb)\n\n## Hypotheses\n- **Media degradation in part of the cohort, showing up as pending and reallocated sectors before failure.** (confidence: medium). How to confirm: Check pending-sector growth in the 60 days before failure for every failed drive, not only the 1% sample, and split the cohort by data center and first-seen month.\n- **An age effect as the cohort passes about four and a half years in service.** (confidence: low). How to confirm: Compare AFR by age band using SMART 9 (power-on hours) and test whether older drives account for the excess failures.\n\n## Recommended actions\n- Prioritise ST12000NM0008 drives with non-zero SMART 197 or 5 for proactive replacement or data migration.\n- Re-run this investigation monthly for the next two quarters to see whether the rate stays above 2026Q1.\n- Ask the operations team for data-center and firmware breakdowns, which the public dataset does not carry.\n\n## Limitations\n- SMART trends come from a 1% sample plus failed drives, not the full cohort.\n- Many failures show no prior SMART signal.\n- Backblaze data: drives that fail on their first day are not captured.\n- Demo only: every number here is synthetic.\n\n## Unverified (failed evidence check)\n- About 41.2% of the drives that failed in 2026Q2 sat in a single data center. (ev_ae356114fe296998)\n"
 },
